    <encodingDesc>
      <appInfo>
        <application n="1" version="0.2.0" xml:id="parseDox2XML.xsl_v0.2.0">
          <name>parseDox2XML.xsl</name>
        </application>
        <application n="2" version="0.9.8" xml:id="doxml2mei.xsl_v0.9.8">
          <name>doxml2mei.xsl</name>
          <p>This converter is a reasonably large collection of XSLT stylesheets (utilizing XSLT 3.0), developed by <persName auth="GND" auth.uri="https://d-nb.info/gnd/174032757" codedval="174032757" xml:id="JoKep">Johannes Kepper</persName> (<ref target="https://edirom.de">
              <corpName auth="GND" auth.uri="https://d-nb.info/gnd/1095564986" codedval="1095564986">ViFE</corpName>
            </ref>) on behalf of and assisted by the <ref target="https://mozarteum.at">Salzburg Mozarteum Foundation</ref> staff, with revisions and extensions by <persName sameas="#FK">Franz Kelnreiter</persName>.</p>
        </application>
        <application n="3" version="1.2.2" xml:id="enhanceFromDoxml.xsl_v1.2.2">
          <name>enhanceFromDoxml.xsl</name>
          <p>The pipeline corrects MEI after the base conversion from DOX using the respective data from doxml. Developed by <persName sameas="#OS">Oleksii Sapov</persName>.</p>
        </application>
        <application version="2.5.2" xml:id="DOXconvX.xsl_v2.5.2">
          <name>DOXconvX.xsl</name>
          <p>This is an extension of the DOX conversion process. Among other corrections, it enriches the encoding by the project specific features. Developed by <persName sameas="#OS">Oleksii Sapov</persName>.</p>
        </application>
        <application version="v1.0" xml:id="mei40To50.xsl">
          <name>mei40To50.xsl</name>
          <ptr target="https://github.com/music-encoding/encoding-tools/blob/main/mei40To50/mei40To50.xsl"/>
        </application>
      </appInfo>
      <editorialDecl label="Editor’s Note" xml:lang="EN">
        <p>This is the encoding of the first movement of K. 280 according to the <rend rend="italic">Neue Mozart-Ausgabe</rend> IX/25/1 (ed. Wolfgang Plath and Wolfgang Rehm, 1986). In the digital edition, this publication is encoded as 'source_1', while the corresponding Critical Report (Wolfgang Rehm, 1998) is listed as 'source_2'. The Critical Report itself is not part of the electronic edition. However, its consultation is possible through NMA Online.</p>
        <p>The NMA edition is based on Mozart’s <ref target="https://jbc.bj.uj.edu.pl/publication/242722" targettype="website">autograph score</ref> (NMA siglum A), held at the Biblioteka Jagiello&#324;ska, Kraków, call number <rend fontstyle="italic">Mus. ms. autogr. W. A. Mozart 279-284.330.455</rend>. For more information on the sources see the <ref target="https://dme.mozarteum.at/DME/nma/nma_cont.php?vsep=198&amp;p1=19&amp;l=1" targettype="website">Critical Report</ref>.</p>
        <p>In the digital edition, the diacritic markup of editorial interventions used in the NMA print edition (i.e. small size, italics, brackets, dashed line form) are coded as additions; as such they are marked as decisions by the editors of the NMA (@resp). Wherever possible the markup of additions affects the smallest unit that can be individually addressed, usually an element. With the exception of possible corrections of obvious printing errors (marked in the code as such) the coding of the musical text is faithful to the aforementioned NMA edition. Corrigenda and addenda listed in the Critical Report have been tacitly taken over. Regarding this movement, however, no corrigenda and addenda are reported. Some normalizations have been applied; for a full description of the editorial principles see the <ref target="https://dme.mozarteum.at/musik/edition/#200" targettype="website">Editorial Guidelines of the DME Music Editions</ref>.</p>
        <p>The present encoding in MEI 4.0.1 (with some minor customizations) is based on a previous encoding in DOX format. The DOX data were gained from optical music recognition (OMR) and proofreading processes developed and carried out by <corpName sameas="#PHI">The Packard Humanities Institute</corpName>, Los Altos, CA.</p>
        <p>For technical reasons, it is possible that some of the original editorial markup not captured by OMR (e.g. small staccato signs) is not represented in the encoded versions.</p>
        <p>Lacunae may be reported to the <ref target="dme@mozarteum.at" targettype="email">Editorial Office of the DME</ref>.</p>
        <p>
          <biblList>
            <head>
              <rend fontweight="bold">Bibliography</rend>
            </head>
            <bibl>Mozart, Wolfgang Amadeus. Neue Ausgabe sämtlicher Werke, Serie IX, Werkgruppe 25: Klaviersonaten Band 1. Ed. Wolfgang Plath and Wolfgang Rehm. Kassel: Bärenreiter, 1986.</bibl>
            <bibl>Rehm, Wolfgang. Wolfgang Amadeus Mozart, Neue Ausgabe sämtlicher Werke, Kritische Berichte, Serie IX, Werkgruppe 25: Klaviersonaten Band 1 und 2. Kassel: Bärenreiter, 1998.</bibl>
          </biblList>
        </p>
        <p>First release: <date isodate="2022-12-12"/>
        </p>
      </editorialDecl>
      <projectDesc>
        <p>The DOX data results from an optical music recognition (OMR) software and proofreading processes developed and realised by <corpName sameas="#PHI">The Packard Humanities Institute, Los Altos, CA</corpName>.</p>
      </projectDesc>
